{
 "id": "2021-kim-signboards",
 "name": "Signboards and Commercial Streetscapes in Seoul",
 "description": "Method replication: participants see 8 street views from five commercial areas of Seoul (Jonggak, Insa-dong, Myeong-dong, Garosu-gil, Gangnam; the area is named under each image) and rate each on the paper's six five-point scales: streetscape satisfaction, signboard satisfaction, aesthetic quality, dull–interesting, still–dynamic and complexity; at the end they tick the streetscape elements that mattered most to them. The images are not the paper's stimuli (30 Google Street View views of the five areas, 2015–2018, fetched for this template).\nOriginal study: Kim & Park (2021), \"The Visual Effect of Signboards on the Vitality of the Streetscapes Using Eye-Tracking\", Sustainability 13(1), 30. The authors photographed the five areas in September 2019 (133 photos, pedestrians removed) and chose 17 photos, split into high and low signboard groups by whether signboards covered more than 10% of the image. 33 participants in their 20s and 30s viewed the photos on an eye tracker, then rated each photo on the scales and checked the most important streetscape elements (signboards, green spaces, facades, street structure, sky, ground floors, other facilities). Photos with more signboard area were rated lower for satisfaction and aesthetics but higher for interesting and dynamic.\nDifferences from the original study: (1) Street View images instead of the authors' photos; pedestrians are not removed, and the images are not grouped by signboard area (the share of signboards can be measured afterwards); (2) no eye tracking; (3) 8 random images per participant instead of all 17; (4) the most important elements are asked once at the end instead of for each photo; (5) the question on offline consumption habits is not included.",
